This paper investigates the standardized existence of a ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
\begin{split}
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
\Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda V(x)u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
\quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
\end{array}
\right.
\end{split} \end{align*} under specific constraints and near a critical point.

Recent analyses of lepton flavor universality (LFU) violating decays have led to constraints on the new physics contributing to $b 	o c 	au  ar{
u}$ transitions. We examine how a leptoquark, integral to these processes, could affect the decay of $\Lambda_b$ particles into a proton, a tau lepton, and an antineutron lepton. Utilizing the established framework of the $U_1$ leptoquark and incorporating the impact of the LFU violating decays, we derive equations that relate the leptoquark's mass and its coupling to the amplitude of the $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ decay. We then explore how the constraint from the $b 	o c 	au  ar{
u}$ analysis affects the possible contributions of the $U_1$ leptoquark to the form factor associated with the $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ decay and estimate the impact on the partial decay width and corresponding branching ratio.
